{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 40,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's not too often that a giant of physics "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2160,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "threatens to overturn an \nidea held to be self-evident"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 5960,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "by generations of physicists."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8280,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, that may be the fate of the famous Penrose\nSingularity Theorem if we're to believe a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14520,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "recent paper by Roy Kerr."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17120,
    "dDurationMs": 12200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Long story short, the terrible singularity\nat the heart of the black hole may be no more."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29320,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A few hundred years ago Isaac Newton\nfigured out how gravity works."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 33400,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Suddenly a lot of mysterious things made a\nlot more sense–from the reason apples fell"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38840,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from trees to the motion of the planets and\nthe stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 42920,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the discovery also birthed a new and stranger\nmystery–it hinted at the possibility that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 49560,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the gravity of a sufficiently dense object\ncould produce an event horizon–a surface"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55200,
    "dDurationMs": 3680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of no escape, able to hold prisoner light\nitself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58880,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It raised the specter of black holes–whose\nparadoxical nature plagues physics to this"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 64680,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 66400,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein’s update to Newtonian gravity seemed\nto confirm the theoretical prospect of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, and it also revealed something\neven more challenging for physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75480,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first solution to the equations of general\nrelativity–the Schwarzschild solution–hinted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82000,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that at the very center of the black hole\nthere is a singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87080,
    "dDurationMs": 6640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this hypothetical point of infinite density\nand infinite gravity, GR comes into terrible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93720,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "conflict with quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96000,
    "dDurationMs": 6280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the size scale of the singularity our two\nsupreme theories of the physical world prove"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 102280,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "themselves mutually incompatible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105640,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many, perhaps most physicists were seriously\nuncomfortable with the idea of black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 110880,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112640,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And they got even more uncomfortable when,\nin 1965, British physicist Sir Roger Penrose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119400,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "showed that singularities really are unavoidable\nin general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124320,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His Penrose singularity theorem–for which\nhe won the 2020 Nobel prize–claims that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 131280,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as long as an event horizon exists, so must\na singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135920,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps the most important implication of\nthe singularity theorem is to show that general"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140400,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "relativity really is in fundamental conflict\nwith quantum mechanics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 145600,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that’s the case, then the only salvation\nfrom the paradox of the singularity is some"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152720,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "greater theory combining quantum mechanics\nand general relativity, in which the singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 158560,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will evaporate away–as if it was just the\nbad dream of ignorant 20th century physics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But just recently we’ve had a ray of hope\nfrom a completely unexpected direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171400,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a paper released in December, Roy Kerr–one\nof the greatest black hole theorists of all"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177000,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time–may have shown that we can avoid the\nblack hole singularity without quantum mechanics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 182480,
    "dDurationMs": 1320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183800,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to get to this radical new result,\nwe need to build up some understanding, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188720,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "let’s refresh our knowledge of black holes\nand review the Penrose singularity theorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196080,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then maybe we can decide if Roy Kerr really\ndid destroy the singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201400,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, to start with Roger Penrose didn’t exactly\nprove the existence of singularities–not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 207920,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "explicitly anyway."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209400,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He demonstrated that spacetime paths must\nterminate inside a black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 215480,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anything moving in spacetime under only gravity\nfollows something called a geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221800,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a path through spacetime that minimizes\nthe combined spatial and temporal distance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226560,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "traveled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228080,
    "dDurationMs": 7320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before the Penrose Singularity Theorem, it\nwas generally held that geodesics had no end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235400,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An object might travel along a segment of\na geodesic–for example, a ball being thrown"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241400,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a parabola–but the geodesic itself can\nbe traced both backwards and forwards beyond"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246400,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ball’s trajectory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247960,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Forward forever into the expanding universe,\nor backwards to the beginning of the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253000,
    "dDurationMs": 7560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roger Penrose showed that inside a black hole,\ngeodesics have to converge at the center and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260560,
    "dDurationMs": 1800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "end there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 262360,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When a spacetime admits geodesics which don’t "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265720,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go on forever we say the \nspacetime is “geodesically"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269680,
    "dDurationMs": 880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "incomplete”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270560,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You can imagine geodesics as the gridlines\nof spacetime, forming a smooth, if sometimes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275840,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quite warped fabric on which the laws of physics\nwork nicely."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281000,
    "dDurationMs": 3400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Geodesic incompleteness \nmeans there are pinched-off "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284400,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regions where infinities appear and the laws"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287160,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of physics break down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 288880,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the argument of Penrose was that \ngeodesic incompleteness means singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 295560,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Kerr has an objection to \nthe argument and it depends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 299800,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on a subtle interpretation \nof geodesic incompleteness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 304160,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s dig a little deeper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306200,
    "dDurationMs": 2680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When Penrose said a geodesic captured by a "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308880,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole “ends” at the \nblack hole center, he meant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 312680,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "something very specific, mathematically."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 315520,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He meant that the “geodesic parameter”\nis bounded–so the mathematical variable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 321680,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we use to describe the evolution of something\nalong a geodesic terminates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327680,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Similar to how your latitude terminates if\nyou travel to the s outh pole–you can’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333760,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "go further south once your southness is maxed\nout."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338480,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For the geodesics describing the paths of\nmatter, the geodesic parameter can be, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 343200,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "usually is, taken to be the “proper time”\n–that’s just the time measured by someone moving"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 349040,
    "dDurationMs": 2040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "along that trajectory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351080,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if the parameter for a matter geodesics is\nbounded that would imply a singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358600,
    "dDurationMs": 3920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because there’s no way to trace a flow of\ntime through it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362520,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s no meaningful way to define “after\nthe singularity”, in either space or time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368560,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are dead-ends in spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 372600,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now Penrose constructed his argument using\nthe paths of light, not of matter, and it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379320,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "turns out the difference is crucial, as we’ll\nsee."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383600,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However the general argument that geodesic\nincompleteness equals a singularity was a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389180,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "convincing enough argument that for nearly\n60 years almost all of us agreed that pure"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395320,
    "dDurationMs": 3440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "general relativity demands singularities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 398760,
    "dDurationMs": 7440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stephen Hawking even used Penrose’s arguments\nto show that in pure GR the Big Bang was also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406200,
    "dDurationMs": 7640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a singularity–all geodesics traced backwards\nin time had to converge and end at one point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413840,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Roy Kerr had his doubts, to put it mildly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418480,
    "dDurationMs": 7080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kerr is a New Zealand physicist who, in 1963,\ncame up with the Kerr metric–the mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425560,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "descriptio n of a rotating black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 427960,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This was the second black hole solution to\nthe Einstein equations to be discovered–47"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 434840,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "years after Karl Schwarzschild \nsolution, and that one just "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 438880,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "describes the much simpler case of a non-rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441880,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443480,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we have good reason to \nbelieve that essentially "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446360,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all real black holes have some rotation, so"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 450120,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Kerr solution is kind of a big deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453320,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As is Roy Kerr."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454880,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Roy Kerr vehemently \ndisagrees that singularities "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 458720,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist, nor even that the Penrose Singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 462320,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Theorem has anything to say about their existence."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466880,
    "dDurationMs": 2720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s finally get to the heart of his objection."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 469600,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I told you that geodesic incompleteness\nhas been taken to mean that spacetime paths"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475000,
    "dDurationMs": 4320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "terminate, which in turn has been taken to\nmean that singularities are real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479960,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s a catch to this argument."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 482440,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose constructed his \nargument using a particular "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485520,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "type of geodesic–the null geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488520,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the spacetime paths traveled by\nmassless, lightspeed objects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493240,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A null geodesic represents the shortest path\nbetween two points in curved space."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 499040,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so what does it mean for a null geodesic\nto terminate?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503760,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It means its geodesic parameter has to be\nbounded and not increase forever."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 508720,
    "dDurationMs": 6200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the case of massive particles we used proper\ntime to trace these regular geodesics."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514920,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But things traveling at the speed of light\ndon’t experience time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 519280,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their clocks remain frozen and so proper time\ndoesn’t increase along a null geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524880,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To describe the geodesic motion of light,\nwe need a different measure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529120,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We use something called an “affine parameter”\nwhich is a slightly complicated thing, but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 535040,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the main thing is that it increases in a nice\nclean way to track progress along a null geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 541960,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Penrose’s theorem shows very convincingly\nthat affine parameters are bounded inside"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 546960,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes, and so null geodesics end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550840,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He then took this to infer the inevitability\nof singularities as dead-ends in the grid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556600,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 558440,
    "dDurationMs": 7160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Kerr points out that these affine parameters\nDON’T track time in a meaningful way, and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565600,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so don’t imply that the grid of spacetime\nfalls apart at the termination of a null geodesic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571680,
    "dDurationMs": 7280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To illustrate this crudely: the affine parameter\ncould be an exponential of coordinate time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 578960,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This function is bounded from below even though\ntime can go from minus to plus infinity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585680,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, that limit of the affine parameter doesn’t\nmean that time itself comes to a halt, argues"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592520,
    "dDurationMs": 1280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Roy Kerr."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593800,
    "dDurationMs": 6920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He also argues that this invalidates any arguments\nabout the inevitability of singularities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600720,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "due to coordinate system dead ends."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603920,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kerr’s paper is quite fun to read."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607280,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He is snarky to put it mildly, excoriating\nthe physics community over and over for blindly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613880,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "following a conclusion that he states is “built\non a foundation of sand”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619000,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I linked the paper in the description for\nyour amusement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 622200,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another important part of Kerr’s argument\nis about the difference between real black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626200,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "holes and the idealized black holes analyzed\nin the Penrose paper."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 630360,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Essentially all–and perhaps literally all–real\nblack holes must have some rotation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 635240,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Real astrophysical black holes will obey the\nKerr metric, not the Schwarzschild, and the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 641560,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same argument can be extended for charged\nblack holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645720,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kerr black holes do not have a point-like\nsingularity in their center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650480,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Kerr metric, the point singularity\nis stretched out into a ring singularity–a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655480,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "looped strand of infinite curvature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658360,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But Kerr insists that even this isn’t a\nreal singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 663160,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other cool thing about the Kerr metric\nis that collapse towards the singularity is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668280,
    "dDurationMs": 4240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not inevitable as it is in the Schwarzschild\nmetric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672520,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s a region just below the Kerr event\nhorizon where collapse really is unavoidable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 678160,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Across the event horizon all paths lead down,\njust like in a Schwarzschild black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 683640,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not to the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 685560,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a rotating black hole, the centrifugal\neffect of the spinning spacetime counteracts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691320,
    "dDurationMs": 7040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "gravity, resulting in this inner region of\nalmost normal spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698360,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the Kerr black hole there’s an inner\nhorizon, and once you cross it you’re free"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702560,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to move in any direction, even back up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 706560,
    "dDurationMs": 2320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s this ring singularity?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 708880,
    "dDurationMs": 3880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kerr implies that it’s a mathematical fiction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 712760,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s just a convenient way to represent\nthe gravitational field generated by a rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 717120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "object."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718120,
    "dDurationMs": 7000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And he suggests that a true collapsed star\nwould exist in an extended, physical form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725120,
    "dDurationMs": 1480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "inside the inner horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727920,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kerr nails down his argument by demonstrating\nthat, contrary to the conclusion of the Penrose"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 734080,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Singularity Theorem, NOT all null geodesics\nterminate at a singularity in the Kerr black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 739960,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole, even if their affine parameter is finite."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 744680,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He reveals these families of geodesics that\npass the inner event horizon of a Kerr black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749840,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole and continue to exist forever and trace\nout really any path inside the black hole"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 755840,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without having to hit the supposed singularity\nand “stop existing”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761080,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is contrary to the previous belief that\nlight crossing any black hole's event horizon"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765840,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has to end up at the supposed singularity\nat the center."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769800,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s if the ring singularity in the Kerr\nblack hole even exists a meaningful entity rather"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 777320,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than a mathematical convenience, as Roy Kerr\nbelieves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 781120,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what does this all really mean for the\nexistence of singularities?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786200,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, it is important to understand that Kerr’s "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788440,
    "dDurationMs": 3360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "argument isn’t necessarily \nsaying that singularities"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791800,
    "dDurationMs": 8040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "don’t exist, it's saying that the conclusions\nof the singularity theorem proof may be incorrect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 799840,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's saying that bounded affine parameters for  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802880,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "null geodesics don’t imply a \nsingularity, contrary to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807840,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common interpretation of the Penrose Singularity\nTheorem."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810960,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not too many physicists really believe that\nblack hole singularities exist, but most thought"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817120,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that we’d have to bring quantum mechanics\ninto the picture to figure out why."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821760,
    "dDurationMs": 5760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s why Kerr’s paper is a surprise–it\nmay give us a path to defeating the singularity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without having to wait for the elusive theory\nof quantum gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 831960,
    "dDurationMs": 5560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s still work to be done to see if\nKerr’s ideas hold up to scrutiny–and we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837520,
    "dDurationMs": 5080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "have no doubt that there’ll be some excited\narguments from both sides."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842600,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in the meantime we now have reason to "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 844800,
    "dDurationMs": 3160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be less scared of the \ninteriors of black holes—from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847960,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a theoretical standpoint."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 849960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Without singularities, perhaps we can start\nformulating sensible physics about what happens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 855480,
    "dDurationMs": 1640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in their interiors."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857120,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Perhaps, with Roy Kerr’s new ideas physicists\ncan travel a bit safer in theoretical space"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 864080,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "thanks to a singularity-free spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868680,
    "dDurationMs": 2760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey Everyone! We wanted to say thank you \nto all the community who support this show,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871440,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether it’s buying merch from the merch store \nor simply liking, commenting and subscribing.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 876080,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in particular we wanted to say thank \nyou to our long time Patreon supporters.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Patreon supporters are a huge reason why we’ve \nbeen able to make the show as it is. So as a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 887080,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "special thank you we’re adding in a permanent \n10% merch store discount to Patreon members  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 893120,
    "dDurationMs": 6440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of all levels and we’ll be opening our next \nPatreon Livestream AMA to all paid patreon  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 899560,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "levels. And of course if you join us on Patreon, \nyou’ll have access to the Space Time Discord  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 905160,
    "dDurationMs": 23440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to talk Space Time 24/7. There’s a link in the \ndescription if you’d like to join, so thank you!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
